// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 4: The relational model
export default {"lead": "<p>The relational model turns each entity into a table and each relationship into a primary key and foreign key pair, then uses integrity rules to keep the tables consistent with each other.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p4-where-it-came-from", "t": "Where it came from", "h": "<p>E.F. Codd, an IBM engineer, introduced the model in a 1970 paper. It is built on mathematics (relational algebra), so its structures are provably correct. The lecture's point: relational projects fail because of how people apply the model, not because of the theory.</p><p>Formally, a relation is any subset of the Cartesian product of its attributes' domains, which is why a row is called a tuple. The instructor has said this level of math will not be tested.</p>"}, {"id": "p4-three-vocabularies-for-the-same-things", "t": "Three vocabularies for the same things", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relational model (correct)</th><th>Spreadsheet habit</th><th>Old file systems</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relation</td><td>Table</td><td>File</td></tr><tr><td>Attribute</td><td>Column</td><td>Field</td></tr><tr><td>Tuple</td><td>Row</td><td>Record</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Know the correct terms, but expect them to be used interchangeably, including by the instructor.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Domain</td><td>The set of allowable values for an attribute</td></tr><tr><td>Degree of a relation</td><td>Its number of columns. Part of the intension; it does not change.</td></tr><tr><td>Cardinality of a relation</td><td>Its number of rows. Part of the extension; it changes constantly.</td></tr><tr><td>Relation schema</td><td>The relation's name followed by its attributes: Student(stuId, lastName, firstName, major, credits)</td></tr><tr><td>Base table</td><td>A table that actually exists in the logical schema</td></tr><tr><td>View</td><td>A virtual table built from base tables, used to give a role just the data it needs or to hide data for security</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Careful: \"degree\" and \"cardinality\" mean different things here than in the ER model, where degree counts entity sets in a relationship and cardinality counts related instances.</p>"}, {"id": "p4-the-seven-properties-of-a-relation", "t": "The seven properties of a relation", "h": "<p><strong>All relations are tables, but not all tables are relations.</strong> A table is a relation only if it has every one of these properties.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Property</th><th>What breaks it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>A row holds facts about one entity instance</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A column holds the values of one attribute of the entity set</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>All values in a column come from the same domain</td><td>Typing \"contact by phone\" in an email address column (a domain integrity violation)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Each column has a unique name</td><td>Two columns both called Name</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Each cell holds one and only one value; no lists, arrays or sets</td><td>Three email addresses typed into one cell (a multivalued attribute)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>The order of rows and of columns carries no meaning</td><td>A member list where \"charter members are at the top\". Fix it with an attribute such as date joined.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>No two rows are identical</td><td>Two completely duplicate rows. Fix it by adding an attribute that always differs, usually a surrogate key.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Property 5 is the one most often violated, and removing its violations is exactly what first normal form does (Part 5).</p>"}, {"id": "p4-keys-in-the-relational-model", "t": "Keys in the relational model", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Superkey</td><td>Any set of attributes that uniquely identifies tuples, including sets with unnecessary extra attributes</td></tr><tr><td>Candidate key</td><td>A superkey with no unnecessary attributes (no proper subset is also a superkey)</td></tr><tr><td>Primary key</td><td>The candidate key chosen to identify tuples. It can be composite. There is exactly one per relation.</td></tr><tr><td>Surrogate key</td><td>An attribute created only to serve as the primary key, usually a meaningless sequence number. Used when no good natural key exists; many organizations require one in every relation.</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign key</td><td>An attribute, or set of attributes, that is the primary key of some relation, called its <strong>parent</strong> or <strong>home</strong> relation. A relation can have any number of foreign keys, including none.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The keys nest: the primary key is one of the candidate keys, and every candidate key is a superkey.</p><p>A good primary key is a <strong>single attribute, short, numeric, and never changes</strong>. If no candidate key fits, create a surrogate key.</p><p>Example, CUSTOMERS(Customer_ID, Email_Address, Full_Name):</p><ul><li>Superkeys include {Customer_ID}, {Email_Address}, {Customer_ID, Full_Name}, and {Customer_ID, Email_Address, Full_Name}.</li><li>Candidate keys are {Customer_ID} and {Email_Address}.</li><li>Primary key: Customer_ID (short, numeric, never changes). Email_Address becomes an alternate key, kept unique with a unique constraint.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p4-integrity-constraints", "t": "Integrity constraints", "h": "<p>Integrity means correctness and internal consistency. An integrity constraint is a rule that applies to every instance of the database; the DBMS rejects any change that would break one.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Domain constraint</td><td>A value must come from the attribute's domain</td><td>A yes/no attribute accepts only two values</td></tr><tr><td>Entity integrity</td><td>No attribute of a primary key can be null</td><td>Every order must have an Order_ID</td></tr><tr><td>Referential integrity</td><td>Every foreign key value must match a primary key value in its home relation, or be completely null</td><td>An order with Customer_ID 128 requires a customer 128 to exist</td></tr><tr><td>General constraints (business rules)</td><td>Any other rule the organization imposes</td><td>Quantity must be greater than zero</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A child row whose parent row is missing is called an <strong>orphan</strong>. Referential integrity exists to prevent orphans.</p><p><strong>Null</strong> means no value. It is not zero and not blank. A <strong>not null</strong> constraint forces a value to be present; Quantity on an order line must be not null, while a supplier's Main_Contact may be left null.</p>"}, {"id": "p4-from-er-diagram-to-tables-the-mapping-rules", "t": "From ER diagram to tables: the mapping rules", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>ER structure</th><th>What it becomes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entity</td><td>A relation. Its identifier is replaced by a proper primary key, sometimes a surrogate.</td></tr><tr><td>One-to-many relationship</td><td>Put the primary key of the parent (the \"one\" side) into the child (the \"many\" side) as a foreign key. Never the other way: the parent would need many values in one cell.</td></tr><tr><td>One-to-one relationship</td><td>Put the primary key of either relation into the other as a foreign key, not both. Rule of thumb: choose the side that produces the fewest null foreign keys.</td></tr><tr><td>Many-to-many relationship</td><td>A new relation holding the primary keys of both entities as foreign keys, plus any data about the relationship. Needed even when there is no extra data.</td></tr><tr><td>Association entity</td><td>Same as above: its relation carries a foreign key to each parent plus its own attributes</td></tr><tr><td>Recursive one-to-one or one-to-many</td><td>A foreign key in the same table that refers to that table's own primary key: Employee(empId, firstName, lastName, managerEmpId)</td></tr><tr><td>Recursive many-to-many</td><td>A separate relation with two foreign keys back to the same table</td></tr><tr><td>Multivalued attribute</td><td>A new relation holding the owner's primary key plus the attribute; the two together form its primary key</td></tr><tr><td>Weak (ID-dependent) entity</td><td>A relation whose primary key includes the owner's primary key plus the partial key</td></tr><tr><td>Composite attribute</td><td>One column per component (street, city, state, zip), or a single column</td></tr><tr><td>Supertype with subtypes</td><td>Usually one relation for the supertype and one per subtype; each subtype's primary key is the supertype's primary key and is also a foreign key to it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The textbook lists three ways to map a supertype and its subtypes:</p><ol><li>A table for the supertype and one for each subtype. Works for every case.</li><li>Subtype tables only, each repeating the common attributes. Do not use it for inclusive (overlapping) subtypes or for partial specialization.</li><li>One big table with every attribute. No joins, but many nulls.</li></ol><p><strong>Count check:</strong> every one-to-many or one-to-one line on the diagram needs one foreign key, and a plain many-to-many line needs two, both in its new table. Five lines and four foreign keys means you missed one.</p>"}, {"id": "p4-worked-example-nsfdb-as-tables", "t": "Worked example: NSFdb as tables", "h": "<p>Key marks used from here on, the same ones you draw by hand on the practicum: a solid underline is a primary key attribute, a dashed underline is a foreign key, and both together mean the attribute is both. The instructor's printed solutions use bold and italic for the same thing.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>Attributes</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CUSTOMERS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk\">Customer_ID</span>, Email_Address, Full_Name</code></td><td>Email_Address is constrained unique (an alternate key)</td></tr><tr><td>ORDERS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk\">Order_ID</span>, Order_Datetime, <span class=\"fk\">Customer_ID</span>, Order_Status</code></td><td>Customer_ID implements \"customers place orders\"</td></tr><tr><td>PRODUCTS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk\">Product_ID</span>, Product_Name, Unit_Price, Product_Details, Product_Image</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ORDER_ITEMS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk\">Line_ID</span>, <span class=\"fk\">Order_ID</span>, Line_Item_ID, <span class=\"fk\">Product_ID</span>, Unit_Price, Quantity</code></td><td>Surrogate primary key Line_ID; (Order_ID, Line_Item_ID) is also unique</td></tr><tr><td>SUPPLIERS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk\">Supplier_ID</span>, Supplier_Name, Main_Contact, Main_Phone, Main_Email</code></td><td>Supplier_Name is constrained unique</td></tr><tr><td>SHIPMENTS</td><td><code class=\"rel\"><span class=\"pk fk\">Line_ID</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">Supplier_ID</span>, Quantity_Shipped, Shipper, Tracking_Num, ShipDate</code></td><td>Association entity. Composite primary key, and each part is also a foreign key.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The ER diagram has five relationship lines and the schema has five foreign keys: ORDERS.Customer_ID, ORDER_ITEMS.Order_ID, ORDER_ITEMS.Product_ID, SHIPMENTS.Line_ID and SHIPMENTS.Supplier_ID.</p>"}, {"id": "p4-reading-a-relational-model-diagram", "t": "Reading a relational model diagram", "h": "<p>The class diagrams come from Oracle Data Modeler and mark each attribute:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P</td><td>Part of the primary key</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>Foreign key</td></tr><tr><td>PF</td><td>Both</td></tr><tr><td>U</td><td>Constrained to be unique (how an unchosen candidate key is kept unique)</td></tr><tr><td>Red asterisk</td><td>Not null</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>On the relationship line, the arrowhead points at the parent and the crow's foot sits on the child.</p><p>Three ways to document the same schema, in increasing detail: the <strong>ER diagram</strong> (conceptual; entities and relationships), the <strong>relational schema map</strong> (relation names with attribute lists and arrows from each foreign key to its primary key; older style), and the <strong>relational model diagram</strong> (boxes with keys, data types and constraints; the modern preference).</p>"}]};
